17] [17592186155854 #edb/ref 1080 "The pen is blue or red." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155854 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155854 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "85acb5a2-dc53-57e3-b0f6-e7940d41e75c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155855 #edb/ref 1080 "The pen is blue or red but not both." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155855 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155855 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c4b7468a-091a-531f-9699-8eaa8f8f5074" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155856 #edb/ref 1080 "The pen is blue and red." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155856 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155856 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e4be91d4-965a-5639-b199-89eaf65b4340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155857 #edb/ref 1080 "The pen is blue." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155857 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155857 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48c558d2-3cd0-5cb1-ac0e-6acf0a3204b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155858 #edb/ref 1080 "The pen is red." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155858 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155858 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5afe80d4-6bcf-5ec7-99fb-14b79fee6f49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155859 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155859 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155859 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "970ba248-4f05-563c-9453-e80422d1588f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155860 #edb/ref 1080 "The marker is purple." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155860 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155860 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "adcb851e-858e-57b0-a3ec-17e8bcca9b7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155861 #edb/ref 1080 "The marker is black but not purple." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155861 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155861 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b0d85b60-eeb9-5495-9f7f-a31118c097c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155862 #edb/ref 1080 "The marker is purple or black." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155862 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155862 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f5533829-6017-5c94-960e-d24b4abc189c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155863 #edb/ref 1080 "The marker is purple and black." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155863 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155863 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11499491-c7c6-5aa9-be33-26ec1ad4f1d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155864 #edb/ref 1080 "The marker is black." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155864 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155864 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6aa73c61-4962-56e4-a7d4-f4d012e90444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157003 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157003 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the sentence \"The car is fancy and big, and the car is big.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a14e5d71-82ca-5df6-8875-f28eece240c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157003 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's rewrite the phrase \"the car is fancy and big\" as the equivalent phrase \"the car is fancy and the car is big.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The car is fancy and the car is big, and the car is big.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The car is fancy, and the car is big and the car is big.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"the car is big and the car is big\" to just \"the car is big.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The car is fancy, and the car is big.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely as\n\n\"The car is fancy and big.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157004 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157004 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $(P \\land Q) \\land (Q \\lor Q).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec2031e2-5f0b-5f12-bf52-1ef26fd84b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157004 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the associative and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∧Q)∧(Q∨Q) & ≡(P∧Q)∧Q \\\\  & ≡P∧(Q∧Q) \\\\  & ≡P∧Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157005 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157005 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the following sentence: \"The table is brown and it is high, and it is brown.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f69673c-7b2d-50ab-9df8-3de7c1933739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157005 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the commutative law, we can replace the phrase \"the table is brown and it is high\" with the phrase \"the table is high and it is brown.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The table is high and it is brown, and it is brown.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The table is high, and it is brown and it is brown.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"it is brown and it is brown\" to just \"it is brown.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The table is high and it is brown.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"The table is high and brown.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157006 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157006 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $(A \\land B) \\land (B \\land A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc7f518c-c6f3-5968-bc59-148277395bf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157006 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the associative, commutative, and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A∧B)∧(B∧A) & ≡A∧(B∧B)∧A \\\\  & ≡A∧B∧A \\\\  & ≡(A∧A)∧B \\\\  & ≡A∧B.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157239 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157239 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157239 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157239 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157239 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157239 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157239 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157239 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Sentences Using the Associative Law" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157239 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157239 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "857d696d-6a3f-5fff-a5e1-b05148f28f55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157240 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Symbolic Statements Using the Associative Law" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157240 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157240 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "331fa12f-a113-5aba-bd6e-5bd5bf62298f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157241 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157241 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157241 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157241 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157241 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157241 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157241 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Sentences Using the Commutative Law" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157241 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157241 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "149709f4-f9d8-529f-87af-a022ec1d46a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157242 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157242 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157242 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157242 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157242 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157242 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157242 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157242 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Symbolic Statements Using the Commutative Law" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157242 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157242 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1e18ca89-35ef-5360-b3a1-8c9496247abb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1045 "Logical Associative and Commutative Laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157487 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1edb2ed5-a24c-5fb1-ac9e-f2c5f14ef8a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157822 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157822 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157822 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157822 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157822 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the following sentence: \"The pen is blue and it is a blue pen, or the pen is blue or it is a red pen.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157822 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ae3ad46-5289-52fe-9fde-b40fc7aa7003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157822 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we simplify the phrase \"the pen is blue and it is a blue pen\" to just \"the pen is blue.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The pen is blue, or the pen is blue or it is a red pen.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma in the sentence:\n\n\"The pen is blue or the pen is blue, or it is a red pen.\"\n\nNow, we simplify the phrase \"the pen is blue or the pen is blue\" to just \"the pen is blue.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The pen is blue, or it is a red pen.\"\n\nFinally, we simplify the sentence to\n\n\"The pen is blue or red.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157823 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157823 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157823 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82723" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157823 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157823 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the following sentence: \"The marker is black or it is purple, or it is black.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157823 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d7ddd93-2035-565c-b8ce-d5d380ba961f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157823 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the commutative law, we can replace the phrase \"the marker is black or it is purple\" with the phrase \"the marker is purple or it is black.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The marker is purple or it is black, or it is black.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The marker is purple, or it is black or it is black.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"it is black or it is black\" to just \"it is black.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The marker is purple, or it is black.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"The marker is purple or black.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158028 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158028 #edb/ref 1061 153690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158028 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d8dc71c-270b-5830-a591-a5450808c4c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158028 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158029 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158029 #edb/ref 1061 153691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158029 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e89e049-ffb3-5480-a3b3-55323e3b4f5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158029 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158030 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158030 #edb/ref 1061 153692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158030 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6d7662d2-2b02-5f6e-8668-bc055379040c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158030 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158031 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158031 #edb/ref 1061 153695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158031 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2c0043a-b347-50c7-90f8-e8e2974c3705" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158031 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158032 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158032 #edb/ref 1061 153698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158032 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c21fc98-e914-5678-8b78-7b6fccb2e021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158032 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158033 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158033 #edb/ref 1061 153699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158033 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "71eb950d-a2bb-508b-a83b-f15317dd21a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158033 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158034 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158034 #edb/ref 1061 153700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158034 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9f2076e-3568-5bf4-a054-9644fa797633" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158034 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158035 #edb/ref 1061 153703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158035 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ef38ea9-0380-5b43-8ce2-a62160452609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158035 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158576 #edb/ref 1064 "The **associative law for conjunctions** states that when we have multiple conjunctions in a row, the placement of parentheses does not matter:\n\n$$(A \\land B) \\land C \\equiv A \\land (B \\land C)$$\n\nWe can prove this equivalence using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\land B\\,$ | $\\, B \\land C\\,$ | $\\, (A \\land B) \\land C\\,$ | $\\, A\\land (B \\land C)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nIn natural language, the associative law for conjunctions amounts to moving a comma between \"and\" statements. For example, the associative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{and}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)} \\quad , \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C}$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{A} \\quad , \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{and}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C)}.$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158576 #edb/ref 1065 7974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158576 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ce01eb47-13d6-5f82-8e3e-f1bd91846d71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158576 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158577 #edb/ref 1064 "The **commutative law for conjunctions** states that we can switch the order of a conjunction:\n\n$$A \\land B \\equiv B \\land A$$\n\nWe can prove this result using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\land B\\,$ | $\\,B \\land A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nIn natural language, the commutative law for conjunctions amounts to swapping two phrases surrounding the word \"and.\" For example, the commutative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{and}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)}$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{and}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the cat is quiet}}_{A)}.$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158577 #edb/ref 1065 7975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158577 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e31a6885-bdfb-5354-8825-aecfd2a54a20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158577 #edb/ref 1067 "The Commutative Law for Conjunctions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158586 #edb/ref 1064 "The **associative law for disjunctions** is similar:\n\n$$(A \\lor B) \\lor C \\equiv A \\lor (B \\lor C)$$\n\nWe can prove this equivalence using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\lor B\\,$ | $\\, B \\lor C\\,$ | $\\, (A \\lor B) \\lor C\\,$ | $\\, A\\lor (B \\lor C)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nIn natural language, the associative law for disjunctions amounts to moving a comma between \"or\" statements. For example, the associative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)} \\quad , \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C}$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{A} \\quad , \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C)}.$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158586 #edb/ref 1065 9053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158586 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c6260eed-7f32-50cb-8351-8a4f61b4f3fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158586 #edb/ref 1067 "The Associative Law for Disjunctions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158587 #edb/ref 1064 "The **commutative law for disjunctions** is similar:\n\n$$A \\lor B \\equiv B \\lor A$$\n\nWe can prove this result using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\lor B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nLikewise, in natural language, the commutative law for disjunctions amounts to swapping two phrases surrounding the word \"or.\" For example, the commutative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)}$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the cat is quiet}}_{A)}.$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158587 #edb/ref 1065 9054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158587 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "995e4380-991c-589d-9001-fc91cbd2d0b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158587 #edb/ref 1067 "The Commutative Law for Disjunctions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378545 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378545 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378545 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378545 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $(P∨Q)∨(Q∨R).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378545 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7611f5de-4aff-57f9-acd6-7bfb622415d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378545 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the associative and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∨Q)∨(Q∨R) & ≡((P∨Q)∨Q)∨R \\\\  & ≡(P∨(Q∨Q))∨R \\\\  & ≡(P∨Q)∨R.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378546 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378546 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378546 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378546 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378546 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378546 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378546 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378546 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186378550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378546 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0b1fac27-c0cd-5308-af4a-258bcdb90eca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378547 #edb/ref 1080 "(P∨Q)∧R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378547 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378547 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "024f757b-b34e-5919-a1c0-4007dfbe0e73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378548 #edb/ref 1080 "(P∧Q)∨R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378548 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378548 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ac752121-76c8-575a-930f-9e019ee9981b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378549 #edb/ref 1080 "(P∧Q)∧R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378549 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378549 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a585d1f-7b32-51e1-b008-e9c9a394440e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378550 #edb/ref 1080 "(P∨Q)∨R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378550 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378550 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f27be41-5974-5971-9b11-5b508a5bf90a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378551 #edb/ref 1080 "P∨R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378551 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378551 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "317bb9c2-0240-5d1d-b8f8-bdef4487074c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378580 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $A∨(C∨(B∧B)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a787032c-44b0-561f-8f0b-52f38953bf0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378580 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the associative and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∨(C∨(B∧B)) & ≡A∨(C∨B) \\\\  & ≡(A∨C)∨B.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378581 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378581 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378581 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378581 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378581 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378581 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378581 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378581 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186378586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378581 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5aabfb69-0432-50db-89ca-330b25cab9fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378582 #edb/ref 1080 "(A∧C)∨B" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "65472356-f9c2-5150-9218-550ac82a0d89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378583 #edb/ref 1080 "A∨C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378583 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378583 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "53533b77-c826-517c-b640-b47e0f9e3c80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378584 #edb/ref 1080 "(A∧C)∧B" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378584 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378584 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e24e92e-e217-5102-a6e8-52ae47fc9d3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378585 #edb/ref 1080 "(A∨C)∧B" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378585 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378585 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "342724a5-4f1f-5147-95e9-39458335051b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378586 #edb/ref 1080 "(A∨C)∨B" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378586 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378586 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "722262c2-a9e9-5588-89b2-8e371cdfc30e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378587 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378587 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378587 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378587 #edb/ref 1071 "Identify the sentence that is equivalent to the following: \"$6$ is divisible by $2$ and it is divisible by $3.$\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378587 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a460320-13cc-5323-976d-d299cba54735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378587 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"$6$ is divisible by $2$ and $3.$\"\n\nUsing the commutative law, we can swap the order of the numbers \"$2$\" and \" $3.$\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$6$ is divisible by $3$ and $2.$\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378588 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378588 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378588 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378588 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378588 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378588 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378588 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378588 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186378590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378588 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6985d158-a011-58b4-8bc9-1e36bb1fde28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378589 #edb/ref 1080 "$6$ is divisible by $3$ or $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378589 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378589 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cbfac57f-020d-52b3-ad37-9821bbf0beb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378590 #edb/ref 1080 "$6$ is divisible by $3$ and $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378590 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378590 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cab0b42d-7e9a-5bb8-87c4-473c1bd37fe6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378591 #edb/ref 1080 "$6$ is divisible by $2$ but not by $3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378591 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378591 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9f3264b9-a08d-53d6-ba63-0d1ac025c8f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378592 #edb/ref 1080 "$6$ is divisible by $3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378592 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378592 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "47565ba1-0f58-565c-8252-46bdce34dc43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378593 #edb/ref 1080 "$6$ is divisible by $3$ but not by $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378593 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378593 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "77967022-afb3-5e7b-a142-02d7cf313bfc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186380250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380249 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380249 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380249 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the following sentence: \"The actor is talented and the actor is successful, and the actor is successful or is a successful actor.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380249 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f240e16-b06d-50d1-bdc6-1009b836ca04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380249 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we simplify the phrase \"the actor is successful or is a successful actor\" to just \"the actor is successful.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The actor is talented and the actor is successful, and the actor is successful.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma in the sentence:\n\n\"The actor is talented, and the actor is successful and the actor is successful.\"\n\nNow, we simplify the phrase \"the actor is successful and the actor is successful\" to just \"the actor is successful.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The actor is talented, and the actor is successful.\"\n\nFinally, we simplify the sentence to\n\n\"The actor is talented and successful.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380250 #edb/ref 1075 #e